N:genkit
D:Route Firebase AI feature work into either direct app/client Firebase AI Logic SDK integration or a server-owned Genkit workflow. Choose client-ai-logic, flow-foundation, tool-and-agent, retrieval-and-prompt, evaluation-and-observability, deployment-runtime, or comparison-or-fallback; route platform operations to firebase-cli and broad framework comparisons to survey.
G:genkit firebase firebase-ai-logic ai-workflows client-sdk flows tool-calling rag evaluation observability cloud-run fullstack backend

U[6]:
  Direct app/client Firebase AI Logic integration with explicit App Check/auth/error and data boundaries
  Server-owned reusable Genkit flow contracts across clients, routes, or jobs
  Tool calling, retrieval, prompt files, typed outputs, evals, and traces
  Client-versus-server ownership decisions before implementation
  Plain provider SDK and route-handler fallbacks when a framework is unnecessary
  Firebase/Cloud Run deployment decisions kept separate from platform CLI operations

S[6]{n,action,details}:
  1,Frame packet,Capture app shape, ownership, current packet, feature need, and delivery pressure
  2,Choose ownership,Pick client-ai-logic, server-genkit, plain-sdk-fallback, or survey-first
  3,Choose mode,Pick one primary operating mode before naming tools or models
  4,Freeze boundary,Define one feature/flow contract and client-visible versus server-only responsibilities
  5,Name next slice,Pick the smallest client wiring, flow, eval, or deployment slice
  6,Route out honestly,Send Firebase operations to firebase-cli and broad framework choice to survey

R[5]:
  Do not force a server flow for a direct client SDK feature
  Keep secrets and protected resources server-side
  Define App Check/auth/errors/abuse boundaries for client-ai-logic
  Prefer one crisp feature or flow boundary over a giant AI bucket
  Sync catalog and compact surfaces when ownership modes change
